<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sancho III of Navarre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 992–1035</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Sancho the Great, Sancho Garcés III, Sancho III of Pamplona · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimenez-dynasty">Jiménez dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sancho III, called the Great, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>) from about 1004 to 1035 and the most powerful ruler of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He brought Sobrarbe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a> under his control, dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> through marriage and held León for a time. His lands were divided among his sons, creating the kingdoms of Navarre, Castile and Aragon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre.jpg" alt="Sancho III of Navarre" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Valdavia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lauda_Sepulcral_SanchoIII_El_Mayor_Navarra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When <b>Sancho III the Great of Navarre</b> died in 1035, his illegitimate son Ramiro I received Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the murder of the last count, García Sánchez, in 1029, Castile passed to <b>Sancho III the Great of Navarre</b>, who gave it to his son Ferdinand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…became effectively autonomous in the mid-tenth century, and in 1037 Ferdinand I, count of Castile and son of <b>Sancho III of Navarre</b>, killed Bermudo III, the last king of the old Leonese line, at the Battle of Tamarón and took the Leonese throne by right of his wife…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s greatest expansion came under <b>Sancho III the Great</b> (1004–1035), who through war, marriage and diplomacy extended his authority over Aragon, Sobrarbe, Ribagorza and, from 1029, Castile, and in 1034 occupied León.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Aragon was ruled from Pamplona until <b>Sancho III</b> died in 1035 and left it to his son Ramiro I, who governed it as a separate realm, the beginning of the Kingdom of Aragon, which later grew into the Crown of Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-aragon">County of Aragon</a></li><li><blockquote>The murder of the young Count García Sánchez in 1029 brought Castile under <b>Sancho III of Navarre</b>, whose son Ferdinand I ruled it and became king of León in 1037.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-castile">County of Castile</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in La Bureba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.6,-3.36&amp;year=1019" aria-label="La Bureba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4469 649 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sancho III of Navarre"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4919 919h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4919 919h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.6,-3.36&amp;year=1019">What was here in 1019? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
